    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nLet's recap some of the rules for exponents.\n\nContent:\n- Product rule: to multiply powers of the same base, add the exponents.\n- Quotient rule: to divide powers of the same base, subtract the exponents.\n- Power rule: to raise a power to another power, multiply the exponents.\n- Power of product rule: to raise the product of two numbers to a power, raise each number to that power, and then multiply.\n- Power of quotient rule: to raise a fraction to a power, raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power.\n\nContent:\nWe can use a combination of these rules to simplify expressions containing exponents. Let's see an example of this.\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Combining the Product and Quotient Rules of Exponents\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\dfrac{5^4\\cdot 5^2}{5^3}] as a base raised to a single number.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we rewrite the numerator using the product rule for exponents:\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{5^4\\cdot 5^2}{5^3}= \\dfrac{5^{4+2}}{5^3} = \\dfrac{5^{6}}{5^3}]\n\nThen, we use the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{5^6}{5^3} = 5^{6-3} = 5^{3}]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (5^{2} \u22c5 5^{4})/(5^{6}) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 5^{2}]\nb. [MATH: (1)/(5)]\nc. [MATH: 1]\nd. [MATH: 5]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(5^{2})]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (6^{3} \u22c5 6^{4})/(6^{5}) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 6^{7}]\nb. [MATH: 6^{2}]\nc. [MATH: 6]\nd. [MATH: 6^{12}]\ne. [MATH: 6^{4}]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Combining Various Product and Quotient Rules of Exponents\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: 4^{8} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{7}{4}\\right)^5] as the product of two exponentiated integers.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we rewrite the second factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n[MATH: 4^{8} \u22c5 ((7)/(4))^{5} = 4^{8} \u22c5 (7^{5})/(4^{5})]\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n[MATH: 4^{8} \\cdot \\dfrac{7^5}{4^5} = \\dfrac{4^8}{4^5} \\cdot 7^5]\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n[MATH: \\dfrac{4^8}{4^5} \\cdot 7^5 =4^{8-5}\\cdot 7^5=4^{3}\\cdot 7^5]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((2)/(5))^{6} \u22c5 (1)/(2^{3}) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (2^{4})/(5^{6})]\nb. [MATH: (2^{3})/(5^{6})]\nc. [MATH: (2)/(5^{6})]\nd. [MATH: (5^{6})/(2^{3})]\ne. [MATH: (2^{6})/(5^{3})]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 5^{4} \u22c5 (5 \u22c5 6)^{2} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 5^{6} \u22c5 6^{2}]\nb. [MATH: 5^{5} \u22c5 6]\nc. [MATH: 5^{5} \u22c5 6^{2}]\nd. [MATH: 5^{2} \u22c5 6^{2}]\ne. [MATH: 5^{2} \u22c5 6]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Combining the Power Rule of Exponents With Other Rules\n\nExample Prompt:\nExpress [MATH: \\left(6^4\\right)^2\\cdot 6] as a base raised to a single power.\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we rewrite the first factor using the power rule for exponents:\n\n[MATH: \\left(6^4\\right)^2=6^{4\\cdot 2}=6^8]\n\nThen we use the product rule for exponents:\n\n[MATH: 6^8 \\cdot 6= 6^8 \\cdot 6^1=6^{8+1}=6^9]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((6)/(4^{2}))^{3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: (6^{3})/(4^{5})]\nb. [MATH: (6)/(4^{12})]\nc. [MATH: (6)/(4^{6})]\nd. [MATH: (6^{3})/(4^{6})]\ne. [MATH: 6^{3} \u22c5 4^{6}]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 3^{4} \u00d7 (3^{2})^{4} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 3^{10}]\nb. [MATH: 3^{12}]\nc. [MATH: 3^{24}]\nd. [MATH: 3^{8}]\ne. [MATH: 3^{4}]"
